# qemu-tool overlay: a 32-node fleet with two of everything per guest.
#
# An overlay on `scale-out`, which stays authoritative -- this file carries
# only the keys that differ. Render and bring up with BOTH directory flags:
#
#   qemu-tool gen-compose --env-file qemu/env/scale-out \
#       --env-file qemu/env/overlay-dense \
#       --output-dir /var/lib/qemu-tool/stacks/dense-fleet
#   qemu-tool compose --stack-dir /var/lib/qemu-tool/stacks/dense-fleet \
#       --env-file qemu/env/scale-out --env-file qemu/env/overlay-dense \
#       --profile metrics up -d
#
# Without --output-dir the render lands on the committed 8-VM stack and CI's
# `Gen Compose Check` goes red; without --stack-dir the compose run brings up
# that committed stack rather than this one, whatever layers it is handed.
#
# ---- What this overlay is for ----------------------------------------------
#
# Three experiments at once, on a 192-core / 976G host:
#
#   two rocjitsu GPUs per guest   -- untested before this fleet
#   two ernics per guest          -- 64 mesh nodes, see the warning below
#   eight NVMe per guest          -- 256 drives across the fleet
#
# Separate from overlay-mi100 rather than layered on it: that one describes a
# known-good 32-VM fleet and is worth keeping runnable while this is in flight.
# The host-specific keys below are duplicated from it deliberately; if a third
# fleet wants them, promote them rather than copying a second time.

VM_COUNT=32
VM_NAME_PREFIX=stebates-dense

# ---- The experiment ---------------------------------------------------------

# 32 x 2 = 64 ernics, which is EXACTLY the 64-node cap in rocm-ernic's
# topology payload and ~1.6x the ~40-node wall where registration was measured
# to stall (AGENTS.md issue 17). gen-compose warns and renders anyway; that is
# the point of this fleet. Expect some workers parked in "Registration
# timeout" restart loops with their guests already dead on "vfio-user: timed
# out waiting for reply", and read the result off
#   docker compose logs ernic-1-1 | grep -c 'Registered node'
# (the mesh manager is device 1 of VM 1; above ERNIC_PER_VM=1 the service
# suffix is <vm>-<device>, while MACs, addresses and GIDs are keyed on the
# GLOBAL nic ordinal -- so VM 2's first NIC is ernic-2-1 on 192.168.100.13.)
# Note that count is only visible at log level info; the manager defaults to
# warn, so an empty grep means the lines are not being emitted, not that
# nothing registered.
# There is also no headroom for a single reconnect: node ids are allocated on
# every connect and never reclaimed (issue 13), so one flapping worker walks
# the fleet off the cap.
ERNIC_PER_VM=2

# No mesh cost -- the GPUs do not join it. The cost is PCIe BAR space: two
# 256M apertures per guest behind their own root ports, on top of two ernics
# and eight NVMe on one q35.
ROCJITSU_PER_VM=2

# Eight per guest, 1024G virtual each but sparse, so this is ~nothing until
# written. They arrive unformatted.
VM_NVME=8

# ---- Host-specific (same values as overlay-mi100) ---------------------------

VM_IMAGES_DIR=/var/lib/qemu-tool/images-stebates
QEMU_TOOL_SRC=/home/AMD/stebates/Projects/qemu-minimal

# The key these guests actually accept, which is NOT the ~/.ssh/id_rsa.pub
# default. They boot from the published batesste-ci-images qcow2, whose
# authorized_keys carries that project's own guest key (SHA256:a56c2m...,
# comment "guest") baked in at image build time -- gen-vm's --backing-image
# path returns before cloud-init, so nothing here ever injected a key of ours.
#
# The private half lives in the batesste-ci-images checkout, which is a
# different repo and so is in none of the places inspect searches: the
# sidecar, <image>.pub, the images dir, images/keys/ and ~/.ssh. Naming it
# here is what makes `qemu-tool ssh` and inspect's Key line find it; without
# it both correctly report "guest accepts no key found locally" and fall
# through to a password prompt.
#
# Point this at your own checkout. A fleet built by vm-ernic-fleet.yml instead
# takes the fleet key in VM_IMAGES_DIR/keys and does not need this.
VM_SSH_KEY_FILE=/home/AMD/stebates/Projects/batesste-ci-images/common/ssh/id_rsa.pub
PROM_DATA_DIR=/var/lib/qemu-tool/stacks/dense-fleet/prom-data
VM_EXTRA_HOSTFWD=tcp::9100-:9100
VM_FILESYSTEM=
LMCACHE_ENABLE=false
LOGS_ENABLE=false
# CONTAINER_USER/CONTAINER_KVM_GID used to be pinned here to one operator's
# ids on one node. qemu-tool compose resolves both from the caller now; pin
# them again only to run as somebody else.

# The sbates-fork flavour, pinned deliberately and not inherited. It is the
# only one carrying the vfio-user P2P DMA patch (AGENTS.md issue 19), which is
# what lets one vfio-user device DMA into another's BAR -- with two GPUs and
# two NICs sharing a guest, that is the interesting case. Same build stamp as
# the golden file's images; _check_image_coherence hard-errors on a mismatch.
QEMU_IMAGE=docker.io/sbates130272/batesste-ci-images-ubuntu-qemu-libvfio-user-sbates-fork:20261030.gea6b921-qemu.f91f120-vfu.3719c3a
ERNIC_IMAGE=docker.io/sbates130272/batesste-ci-images-ubuntu-rocm-ernic-sbates-fork:20261030.gea6b921-ernic.8f35f4a-vfu.3719c3a
